---
import BlogLayout from "../../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import { blogPosts } from "../../../lib/blogPosts";

const post = blogPosts.find((p) => p.slug === "execution-process-overview")!;
---

<BlogLayout
  locale="en"
  path="/en/blog/execution-process-overview"
  altPath="/blog/execution-process-overview"
  title={post.titleEn}
  description={post.excerptEn}
  publishDate={post.publishDate}
>
  <p>
    It's often assumed that a court judgment automatically means the prevailing party receives what they're owed —
    in reality, a judgment only marks the end of one phase and the start of an entirely separate one: execution.
  </p>

  <h2>A judgment doesn't enforce itself</h2>
  <p>
    Once a judgment is issued and becomes final (after any appeal or cassation stages are exhausted), the prevailing
    party must file an execution request with the competent court, attaching a copy of the final judgment. Actual
    execution proceedings do not begin automatically without this request.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens once the execution request is accepted?</h2>
  <p>
    The court takes steps to compel the judgment debtor to comply — this may start with direct notice, and escalate
    to firmer measures if there's no response, depending on the nature of the judgment (monetary, delivery of
    property, etc.) and the debtor's conduct.
  </p>

  <h2>What if execution stalls?</h2>
  <p>
    An execution request can run into obstacles — such as being unable to locate the debtor's assets, or the debtor
    not responding to notices. This is exactly what the "Judiciary & Execution" services on the platform cover: you
    can describe your case and receive price offers from specialized lawyers to pursue it, and negotiate the price
    before committing to any offer.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every execution case differs depending on the type of judgment and the other party's posture. For an accurate
    assessment of your case, submit your request under the "Judiciary & Execution" category and you'll receive
    offers from licensed lawyers you can negotiate with directly.
  </p>
</BlogLayout>
